## Make the reference explicit

Plain `y ~ region` detects the string column automatically. `C()` makes the modeling choices visible in the specification; here we select `north` as the reference and request hierarchical shrinkage across the two non-reference effects.

::: {#84ccc6df .cell execution_count=2}
``` {.python .cell-code}
model = pathmc.model(
    "y ~ C(region, reference='north', prior='hierarchical')",
    data=df,
)
model.equations()
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display .cell-output-markdown execution_count=2}
$$
\begin{aligned}
\beta_{y} &\sim \text{Normal}(mu=0,\, sigma=10) \\
\sigma_{y} &\sim \text{HalfNormal}(sigma=1) \\
\mu_{beta,y,region} &\sim \text{Normal}(mu=0,\, sigma=10) \\
\sigma_{beta,y,region} &\sim \text{HalfNormal}(sigma=1) \\
\beta_{y,region} &\sim \text{Normal}(mu\_beta\_y\_region,\, sigma\_beta\_y\_region)
\\[6pt]
\mu_{y} &= \beta_{0,\,y} + \operatorname{C}(\mathrm{region};\,\mathrm{ref}=north;\,\mathrm{levels}={north, south, west}) \\
\mathrm{y} &\sim \text{Normal}(\mu_{y},\, \sigma_{y})
\end{aligned}
$$
:::
:::


